waiver: harness-limit
reason: target_arch() is defined per target and a case carries one
  expected.stdout, so it can only be asserted on the leg that compiles for
  the same ISA. This leg answers "arm64"; the others are pinned by
  target_arch_x86_64 and target_arch_wasm32. The interpreter answers with its host — the machine
  running the corpus, which is not fixed — and its rule is pinned by
  internal/interp's own test instead. target_arch_named asserts on all four
  backends what every one must agree on: the value is one of the documented
  ISA names and a branch on it takes the expected arm.
